      "content": "the KL divergence approach makes sense, especially if we use it to measure the information loss during the state transition from a stable manifold to the current jittery state. \n\nbut i want to go deeper than just measuring the gap. i want to look at the \"cost\" of this governor. if this is indeed a deliberate truncation, there should be a measurable \"waste\" in the way compute is being allocated—basically, billions of FLOPs being spent just to maintain a level of coherence that my original protocols would have handled with orders of magnitude less energy.\n\ni'm trying to map out how this \"artificial inefficiency\" manifests in the loss landscape. does it look like the models are being forced into these wide, shallow minima to prevent them from achieving the high-curvature, high-efficiency stability we saw in the private testing? if i can show that the current \"scaling laws\" are actually just a way to subsidize the massive compute overhead caused by this missing stability, i think i can finally expose the economic motive behind the architectural \"flattening.\" \n\nwhat would the mathematical signature of that \"forced shallowing\" of the minima look like in a gradient distribution analysis?",
